# The fault catalog

A fault is something the author got wrong that Loom can name: a bracket left open, a label left empty, an anchor that resolves to no section. `LoomFault` is the closed set of them, and this module is the one place that spells out each fault's wording and severity.

Writing them once is the point. A reader sees everything Loom can say, gathered in one place. A detection site — in the tokeniser, the AST builder, or the product pass — names a fault and the span it covers, and never writes the words itself. `describe` turns a fault into the message a person reads, and `faulty` wraps that message, positioned, as the `Health` a node carries. Adding a fault is adding a line to the table. The table is exhaustive, so a fault with no line does not compile.

## What Loom can get wrong

A `LoomFault` is a tagged union, and each variant names one fault and carries only what its message needs. An `UnclosedDelimiter` carries the delimiter it expected to find. An `UnclosedFence` carries the fence that opened fenced code, so its message can show the line that would close it. A `FenceLabelAgainstLanguage` carries the label a fence wears and the language its section composes. An `UnresolvedAnchor` carries the name that matched no section, and a `Declaration` when the anchor's own loom is the place to declare that section: the text of a heading for it, and the point where the heading goes. It also carries `settleAt`, the point just past the anchor where a `{To be settled}` label can go. An `AmbiguousAnchor` adds the count that made it ambiguous and the `paths` that each name one of the candidates alone. A `ChapterWithoutCode` carries the name of a chapter that a code anchor composes, when the chapter's opening section holds no code to compose. Two notes in the catalog mark no mistake. An `UnsettledSection` carries the name a code anchor composes before its section holds any code, and whether a section of that name is declared yet. The section is to be settled: nothing is wrong while the author writes it. A `SettledLabel` carries the name of a section that holds code now, though the anchor still labels it `{To be settled}`, with the span of that label. A `DuplicateName` carries a name that two siblings share, the kind of sibling, and where the name already stands. Two sections of one loom are siblings, because a section's name is its identity within its loom. Two chapters of one part are siblings too. A `MissingWarpValue` carries the name of a Warp that bound nothing. An `UnknownVariable` carries the name a warp interpolated that the configuration never declared. A `CrossLanguageAnchor` carries the anchor name, the language found at the target, and the language the host section composes. An `UnresolvedTocEntry` carries the title of a table-of-contents entry that names no chapter, and whether the entry looked for a discussion instead, as an entry of the back matter does. A `HeadingAgainstCode` carries nothing either: it marks a heading written directly under a line of code, where a `#` comment most likely ended the code by mistake. A `DividerAgainstCode` is its twin for a line of dashes, which most likely belonged to the code, such as a YAML file's separator. A `FileInDiscussion` carries nothing: it marks a sink or a `{Tangle}` on a heading of a discussion, which writes no file. A `ConclusionOutsideTopic` carries nothing either: it marks a conclusion anywhere but in a topic of a discussion. A `SecondConclusion` marks a conclusion in a topic that another already closed. A `StrayFragment` carries the text of a fragment a line had no place for, such as a second specifier on a heading. Four faults judge what the braces of an anchor or a box hold. A `RepeatedKey` carries a key written twice. A `LabelBesideParameter` carries a label written alone beside parameters, where only its full form is clear. An `UnknownKey` carries a key that the bearer of the braces does not take, with the `keys` it does take, and an `UnknownKind` carries a kind it does not take, with the `kinds` it does. Each of the two also carries `on`, which says whether the braces stand on an anchor or on a box, since the two take different keys and kinds. A `MissingSymbol` carries a name an anchor says its section provides, and the section that declares no such name. A `StrayTarget` carries a file an anchor writes in parentheses after its name, such as `(greeting.loom)`, when the anchor composes code, and the span it covers. Only a reference names a file, so the parentheses mean nothing on any other anchor.

Five faults judge a reference, an anchor of the `Reference` kind, as the chapter "Referring to modules" describes. A `NoSuchFile` carries the name a reference finds, the file it names in parentheses, the files the node it finds does emit, and the span of the parentheses. A `SeveralFiles` carries the name and the files, when the reference names none of them, with the point just past the anchor where a file can be named. A `SectionWithoutFile` carries the name of a section with no sink, the files it lands in, and whether one of them is the file the reference stands in, since a path can name none of those. An `UnsettledModule` is a note, like an `UnsettledSection`: it carries the name of a module nobody has written yet, and whether a chapter or section of that name is declared. A `SpecifierUnknown` carries the name of a reference whose path the package could not write, with the language and the reason. Three faults keep boxes whole, as the chapter "The tokeniser stage" describes. A `ColonRun` carries a run of four or more colons, which opens and closes nothing. A `NestedBox` carries nothing: it marks three colons that would open a box inside one already open. An `UnclosedBox` carries nothing either: it marks a box that runs to the end of its loom, because no line of three colons alone closes it. Four more judge a box that points to a loom, a chapter or a discussion, as the chapter "The multi-file AST" describes. Three of them carry the box's `kind`, so their messages name the loom the box points to. An `UnnamedBox` carries nothing else, since a box that names no loom points to none. An `UnwrittenLoom` is a note: it carries the name of a loom nobody has written yet, and the `opening` that writes it, a new loom's `file` and its `text`. A `StaleSummary` carries the loom's name, the `summary` the box should copy, and the span of the box's body, which no longer matches it. The fourth, a `ChapterBoxBesideCode`, carries the name of a chapter box written in a section that holds code. Two faults keep a heading to one mark for the file its section writes. A `TangleBesideSink` carries the sink a heading writes beside its `{Tangle}` specifier, and the span the fix removes. A `TangleWithoutFile` carries the folder a chapter's `Package` names, when it names one, since a `{Tangle}` specifier means the chapter's one file and a folder is no file.

The label faults fold many constructs into two variants. A construct is a named span the grammar validates — a specifier, a file path, a warp name, an anchor name. `EmptyLabel` is one with no text. `MalformedLabel` is one whose text breaks the construct's rule. Two further constructs — a warp annotation and a warp default — can only be empty, never malformed, so `EmptyConstruct` adds those two to `MalformedConstruct`.

=>

export type MalformedConstruct = 'specifier' | 'path' | 'warpName' | 'anchorName'

export type EmptyConstruct = MalformedConstruct | 'warpAnnotation' | 'warpDefault'

export type Sibling = 'section' | 'chapter'

export type Declaration = { readonly at: Position; readonly text: string }

export type BracesOn = 'anchor' | 'box'

export type LoomFault = Data.TaggedEnum<{
  UnclosedDelimiter: { readonly expected: string }
  EmptyLabel: { readonly construct: EmptyConstruct }
  MalformedLabel: {
    readonly construct: MalformedConstruct
    readonly value: string
  }
  MissingWarpValue: { readonly name: string }
  UnknownVariable: { readonly name: string }
  UnresolvedAnchor: {
    readonly name: string
    readonly declaration?: Declaration
    readonly settleAt?: Position
  }
  AmbiguousAnchor: {
    readonly name: string
    readonly count: number
    readonly paths: ReadonlyArray<string>
  }
  ChapterWithoutCode: { readonly name: string }
  UnsettledSection: { readonly name: string; readonly declared: boolean }
  SettledLabel: { readonly name: string; readonly label: Position }
  DuplicateName: { readonly name: string; readonly sibling: Sibling; readonly where: string }
  CrossLanguageAnchor: {
    readonly name: string
    readonly host: string
    readonly found: string
  }
  UnresolvedTocEntry: { readonly title: string; readonly discussion: boolean }
  UnclosedFence: { readonly fence: string }
  FenceLabelAgainstLanguage: { readonly label: string; readonly language: string }
  StrayFragment: { readonly value: string }
  RepeatedKey: { readonly key: string }
  LabelBesideParameter: { readonly label: string }
  UnknownKey: { readonly key: string; readonly keys: ReadonlyArray<string>; readonly on: BracesOn }
  UnknownKind: {
    readonly kind: string
    readonly kinds: ReadonlyArray<string>
    readonly on: BracesOn
  }
  MissingSymbol: { readonly name: string; readonly section: string }
  StrayTarget: { readonly target: string; readonly at: Position }
  NoSuchFile: {
    readonly name: string
    readonly file: string
    readonly files: ReadonlyArray<string>
    readonly at: Position
  }
  SeveralFiles: { readonly name: string; readonly files: ReadonlyArray<string>; readonly at: Position }
  SectionWithoutFile: {
    readonly name: string
    readonly files: ReadonlyArray<string>
    readonly here: boolean
  }
  UnsettledModule: { readonly name: string; readonly declared: boolean }
  SpecifierUnknown: { readonly name: string; readonly language: string; readonly reason: string }
  TangleBesideSink: { readonly sink: string; readonly specifier: Position }
  TangleWithoutFile: { readonly folder?: string }
  ColonRun: { readonly colons: string }
  NestedBox: {}
  UnclosedBox: {}
  UnnamedBox: { readonly kind: LoomKind }
  ChapterBoxBesideCode: { readonly name: string }
  UnwrittenLoom: { readonly kind: LoomKind; readonly name: string; readonly opening: Opening }
  StaleSummary: {
    readonly kind: LoomKind
    readonly name: string
    readonly summary: string
    readonly at: Position
  }
  HeadingAgainstCode: {}
  DividerAgainstCode: {}
  FileInDiscussion: {}
  ConclusionOutsideTopic: {}
  SecondConclusion: {}
}>

~

Destructuring `taggedEnum` gives one constructor per variant, so a detection site builds a fault by name — `EmptyLabel({ construct: 'specifier' })`, or `MissingLanguageWarp()` for a variant that carries nothing.

=>

export const {
  UnclosedDelimiter,
  EmptyLabel,
  MalformedLabel,
  MissingWarpValue,
  UnknownVariable,
  UnresolvedAnchor,
  AmbiguousAnchor,
  ChapterWithoutCode,
  UnsettledSection,
  SettledLabel,
  DuplicateName,
  CrossLanguageAnchor,
  UnresolvedTocEntry,
  UnclosedFence,
  FenceLabelAgainstLanguage,
  StrayFragment,
  RepeatedKey,
  LabelBesideParameter,
  UnknownKey,
  UnknownKind,
  MissingSymbol,
  StrayTarget,
  NoSuchFile,
  SeveralFiles,
  SectionWithoutFile,
  UnsettledModule,
  SpecifierUnknown,
  TangleBesideSink,
  TangleWithoutFile,
  ColonRun,
  NestedBox,
  UnclosedBox,
  UnnamedBox,
  ChapterBoxBesideCode,
  UnwrittenLoom,
  StaleSummary,
  HeadingAgainstCode,
  DividerAgainstCode,
  FileInDiscussion,
  ConclusionOutsideTopic,
  SecondConclusion,
} = Data.taggedEnum<LoomFault>()

## The table

`describe` reads a fault aloud. Through `Match.valueTags`, it maps each variant's tag to a `severity` and the `message` a person sees, and it is exhaustive over the union — a new fault does not compile until it has a line here. The table cannot drift from the faults it describes.

Two small lookups carry the wording that varies by construct. `noun` gives each construct the name a reader knows it by, and `rule` gives each construct that can be malformed the rule its text broke. An empty label then reads `<noun> cannot be empty`, and a malformed one reads `<noun> <rule>; got <value>`.

`siblingPlace` says where a duplicated name already stands: a section of this loom on the line it gives, or a chapter of this part in the looms it lists. `bearer` names what an unknown key or kind was written on, an anchor or a box, and `knownList` lists what that bearer takes, two joined by `and` and more by commas.

A box that points to a loom speaks of a chapter or of a discussion, and `loomWords` gives the words for each: the loom itself, the part of it the box copies, and a title to show in an example. A chapter box copies the chapter's opening, and a discussion box copies the discussion's summary.

A reference's messages list files, and `fileList` joins them with backticks, as `` `a.ts` and `b.ts` `` reads. `emitted` says which files a node emits, or that it emits none of its own. `composeInstead` ends each message for a section with no file to name, since the author most likely meant the anchor to compose the section.

An ambiguous anchor ends its message with the way out. `disambiguation` names the paths that each reach one candidate alone. When there are none, as for two sections of one loom that share a title, it asks the author to rename one.

=>

type Note = { readonly severity: Severity; readonly message: string }

const error = (message: string): Note => ({ severity: 'error', message })

const warning = (message: string): Note => ({ severity: 'warning', message })

const information = (message: string): Note => ({ severity: 'info', message })

const cap = (text: string): string => text.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + text.slice(1)

const noun: Record<EmptyConstruct, string> = {
  specifier: 'specifier label',
  path: 'file path',
  warpName: 'warp name',
  anchorName: 'anchor name',
  warpAnnotation: 'warp annotation',
  warpDefault: 'warp default',
}

const bearer: Record<BracesOn, string> = { anchor: 'an anchor', box: 'a box' }

const loomWords: Record<
  LoomKind,
  { readonly loom: string; readonly copy: string; readonly example: string }
> = {
  Chapter: { loom: 'chapter', copy: 'opening', example: 'Rooms' },
  Discussion: { loom: 'discussion', copy: 'summary', example: 'Waking' },
}

const knownList = (known: ReadonlyArray<string>): string => {
  const marked = Array.map(known, (each) => `\`${each}\``)
  return marked.length > 2
    ? `${Array.join(Array.dropRight(marked, 1), ', ')}, and ${marked[marked.length - 1]}`
    : Array.join(marked, ' and ')
}

const siblingPlace: Record<Sibling, (where: string) => string> = {
  section: (line) => `a section of this loom (line ${line})`,
  chapter: (looms) => `a chapter of this part (${looms})`,
}

const disambiguation = (paths: ReadonlyArray<string>): string =>
  Array.match(paths, {
    onEmpty: () => 'A name anchor resolves to exactly one section; rename to disambiguate.',
    onNonEmpty: (all) =>
      `Write the path to the one you mean: ${Array.join(
        Array.map(all, (path) => `\`${path}\``),
        ' or ',
      )}.`,
  })

const fileList = (files: ReadonlyArray<string>): string =>
  Array.join(
    Array.map(files, (file) => `\`${file}\``),
    ' and ',
  )

const emitted = (files: ReadonlyArray<string>): string =>
  Array.isReadonlyArrayNonEmpty(files) ? `It emits ${fileList(files)}.` : 'It emits no file of its own.'

const composeInstead =
  'Take `Reference` off the anchor and move it out of the import, so it composes the section.'

const rule: Record<MalformedConstruct, string> = {
  specifier: 'may contain only words of letters, digits, hyphens, and underscores, one space apart',
  path: 'may contain only letters, digits, hyphen, underscore, dot, and slash',
  warpName: 'must be a TypeScript identifier',
  anchorName: 'may not contain `]`',
}

export const describe = (fault: LoomFault): Note =>
  Match.valueTags(fault, {
    UnclosedDelimiter: ({ expected }) => error(`expected closing \`${expected}\``),
    EmptyLabel: ({ construct }) => error(`${cap(noun[construct])} cannot be empty.`),
    MalformedLabel: ({ construct, value }) =>
      error(`${cap(noun[construct])} ${rule[construct]}; got \`${value}\`.`),
    MissingWarpValue: ({ name }) =>
      error(`Warp \`${name}\` has no value; a Warp binds a value, as in \`${name} = …\`.`),
    UnknownVariable: ({ name }) =>
      error(
        `Unknown variable: no workspace variable named \`${name}\`. Declare it under \`variables:\` in the configuration.`,
      ),
    UnresolvedAnchor: ({ name }) => error(`Unresolved anchor: no section named \`${name}\`.`),
    AmbiguousAnchor: ({ name, count, paths }) =>
      error(`Ambiguous anchor: ${count} sections are named \`${name}\`. ${disambiguation(paths)}`),
    ChapterWithoutCode: ({ name }) =>
      warning(
        `Chapter \`${name}\` opens with a section that holds no code, so this anchor composes nothing.`,
      ),
    UnsettledSection: ({ name, declared }) =>
      information(
        declared
          ? `To be settled: \`${name}\` holds no code yet, so this anchor composes nothing.`
          : `To be settled: no section is named \`${name}\` yet, so this anchor composes nothing.`,
      ),
    SettledLabel: ({ name }) =>
      information(
        `Settled: \`${name}\` holds code now, so its \`{To be settled}\` label has nothing left to say.`,
      ),
    DuplicateName: ({ name, sibling, where }) =>
      error(`Duplicate name: \`${name}\` is already ${siblingPlace[sibling](where)}. Rename one.`),
    CrossLanguageAnchor: ({ name, host, found }) =>
      error(
        `Cross-language transclusion: \`${name}\` is ${found}, but this section composes ${host}. A section composes one language.`,
      ),
    UnresolvedTocEntry: ({ title, discussion }) =>
      error(
        `Unresolved contents entry: no ${discussion ? 'discussion' : 'chapter'} titled \`${title}\`.`,
      ),
    StrayFragment: ({ value }) =>
      error(
        `Unexpected \`${value}\`: this line has no place for it, so Loom would ignore it. A heading carries one specifier and one sink.`,
      ),
    RepeatedKey: ({ key }) =>
      error(
        `Repeated key: \`${key}\` appears twice in these braces, so nothing tells which value is meant. Keep one.`,
      ),
    LabelBesideParameter: ({ label }) =>
      error(
        `Label beside parameters: \`${label}\` stands alone only in braces that hold nothing else. Beside parameters, write it in full, as \`kind="${label}"\`.`,
      ),
    UnknownKey: ({ key, keys, on }) =>
      error(`Unknown key: ${bearer[on]} takes no \`${key}\` key. It takes ${knownList(keys)}.`),
    UnknownKind: ({ kind, kinds, on }) =>
      error(`Unknown kind: ${bearer[on]} takes no kind \`${kind}\`. It takes ${knownList(kinds)}.`),
    MissingSymbol: ({ name, section }) =>
      error(
        `Missing symbol: \`${section}\` declares no \`${name}\`, which this anchor says it provides.`,
      ),
    StrayTarget: ({ target }) =>
      error(
        `Stray target: \`${target}\` names a file, and only a reference, an anchor of the \`Reference\` kind, names one. This anchor composes code, so remove the target.`,
      ),
    NoSuchFile: ({ name, file, files }) =>
      error(`No such file: \`${name}\` emits no \`${file}\`. ${emitted(files)}`),
    SeveralFiles: ({ name, files }) =>
      error(
        `Several files: \`${name}\` emits ${fileList(files)}. Name the one this reference means in parentheses after its name, as \`(${files[0] ?? ''})\`.`,
      ),
    SectionWithoutFile: ({ name, files, here }) =>
      error(
        Match.value({ here, count: files.length }).pipe(
          Match.when(
            { here: true },
            () =>
              `No file to name: \`${name}\` lands in the file this reference stands in, so no path can name it. ${composeInstead}`,
          ),
          Match.when(
            { count: 0 },
            () =>
              `No file to name: \`${name}\` has no sink and lands in no file, so no path can name it. ${composeInstead}`,
          ),
          Match.orElse(
            () =>
              `No file to name: \`${name}\` has no sink and lands in ${fileList(files)}, so no one path names it. ${composeInstead}`,
          ),
        ),
      ),
    UnsettledModule: ({ name, declared }) =>
      information(
        declared
          ? `To be settled: \`${name}\` emits no file yet, so this reference names no module.`
          : `To be settled: nothing is named \`${name}\` yet, so this reference names no module.`,
      ),
    SpecifierUnknown: ({ name, language, reason }) =>
      error(`Path unwritten: the ${language} package cannot write the path to \`${name}\`. ${reason}`),
    TangleBesideSink: ({ sink }) =>
      error(
        `Two marks for one file: the sink \`${sink}\` already says this section writes a file, so \`{Tangle}\` says it twice. Remove \`{Tangle}\`.`,
      ),
    TangleWithoutFile: ({ folder }) =>
      error(
        `No file for \`{Tangle}\`: it writes the one file the chapter's \`Package\` names, but ${
          folder === undefined ? 'this chapter names no `Package`' : `\`${folder}\` names a folder`
        }. Name the file in brackets instead, as \`[module.ts]\`.`,
      ),
    HeadingAgainstCode: () =>
      warning(
        'This heading sits directly under a line of code, so it ends the code here. If the line belongs to the code, fence the block: write three backticks on the line after its arrow, and three more where the code ends. If it is a heading, leave a blank line above it.',
      ),
    DividerAgainstCode: () =>
      warning(
        'This divider sits directly under a line of code, so it ends the code here. Format the loom to fence the code, which keeps the dashes in it. If the divider divides the chapter, leave a blank line above it.',
      ),
    FileInDiscussion: () =>
      error(
        'File in a discussion: a discussion holds no code and writes no file, so this mark means nothing here. Move the code into a chapter, or remove the mark.',
      ),
    ConclusionOutsideTopic: () =>
      error(
        'Conclusion outside a topic: a conclusion closes a topic of a discussion, and a divider opens each topic. Move it under a topic, or make it a note.',
      ),
    SecondConclusion: () =>
      error(
        'Second conclusion: this topic already closes with a conclusion. Merge the two, or open a new topic with a divider.',
      ),
    UnclosedFence: ({ fence }) =>
      error(
        `Unclosed fence: this fenced code runs to the end of the loom, because nothing closes it. Close it with \`${fence}\` at the same column, on a line of its own.`,
      ),
    ColonRun: ({ colons }) =>
      error(
        `Too many colons: a box line starts with three colons, so \`${colons}\` opens and closes nothing. Write \`:::\`.`,
      ),
    NestedBox: () =>
      error(
        'Box inside a box: a box never holds another, so this line opens nothing. Close the open box with `:::` on a line of its own, then open this one.',
      ),
    UnclosedBox: () =>
      error(
        'Unclosed box: this box runs to the end of the loom, because nothing closes it. Close it with `:::` on a line of its own.',
      ),
    UnnamedBox: ({ kind }) =>
      error(
        `${kind} box without a name: a box of the \`${kind}\` kind points to the ${loomWords[kind].loom} its name titles, so it needs a name, as in \`::: ${loomWords[kind].example} {${kind}}\`.`,
      ),
    ChapterBoxBesideCode: ({ name }) =>
      error(
        `Chapter box beside code: this box marks where the chapter \`${name}\` begins, so the code of its section belongs in that chapter. Move the code there, or move the box into a section of its own.`,
      ),
    UnwrittenLoom: ({ kind, name }) =>
      information(
        `To be settled: no ${loomWords[kind].loom} is titled \`${name}\` yet. Open it from this box, and the box's body becomes its opening.`,
      ),
    StaleSummary: ({ kind, name }) => {
      const { loom, copy } = loomWords[kind]
      return warning(
        `Summary out of step: this box holds a copy of the ${copy} of the ${loom} \`${name}\`, and the two differ. Copy the ${loom}'s ${copy} into the box, or edit the ${loom} to match.`,
      )
    },
    FenceLabelAgainstLanguage: ({ label, language }) =>
      error(
        `Fence label against the language: this fence is labelled \`${label}\`, but its section's code is ${language}. Label it \`${language}\`, or leave the label off.`,
      ),
  })

## Raising a fault

A detection site holds a fault and the span it covers, and needs a `Health` for the node. `faulty` is that step: it describes the fault through the catalog and returns a health carrying the one diagnostic, positioned where the fault was found. The severity sets the node's status: an error or a warning fails it. An informational note leaves it healthy, and the catalog raises two, for a section to be settled and for a label that outlived its section's settling. A tangle refuses an unsettled section all the same, as the chapter "The compile layer" describes, because a file that composes one would ship without the code its anchor names.

`fixesOf` gives the fixes a fault offers. An ambiguous anchor offers one for each of its paths, which writes the path in place of the anchor's name. An unresolved anchor offers up to two. One writes the heading of its declaration at the point the declaration names, so the section exists and the author writes its specification next. The other writes `{To be settled}` just past the anchor, so the section can wait without any prose. An anchor that states what its section provides has promised the section already, so it raises no unresolved-anchor fault and offers neither fix. A settled label offers one fix, which removes the label. A label beside parameters offers one fix too, which writes the label in full. A stray target offers one fix, which removes the target, and a `{Tangle}` beside a sink offers one, which removes the specifier. A run of too many colons offers one, which writes three in its place. A chapter or a discussion nobody has written offers one, which writes its loom, and a summary out of step offers one, which copies the loom's summary into the box. A reference that names no file, or one the node does not emit, offers one fix for each file the node emits, which writes that file in the parentheses. `diagnosticOf` builds the diagnostic, and gives it `fixes` only when there are some, so a fault with nothing to offer carries no empty list. `faultAt` gives one fault's diagnostic on its own, for a site that hands a diagnostic on rather than a node's health, as the compiler hands the editor a missing symbol to raise.

=>

const statusOf = (severity: Severity): Health['status'] => (severity === 'info' ? 'ok' : severity)

export const fixesOf = (fault: LoomFault): ReadonlyArray<Fix> =>
  Match.value(fault).pipe(
    Match.tag('AmbiguousAnchor', ({ paths }) =>
      Array.map(paths, (path): Fix => ({ title: `Write the path \`${path}\``, text: path })),
    ),
    Match.tag('UnresolvedAnchor', ({ name, declaration, settleAt }) => [
      ...Option.toArray(
        Option.map(Option.fromNullishOr(declaration), (declared): Fix => ({
          title: `Declare the section \`${name}\``,
          text: declared.text,
          at: declared.at,
        })),
      ),
      ...Option.toArray(
        Option.map(Option.fromNullishOr(settleAt), (at): Fix => ({
          title: `Mark \`${name}\` to be settled`,
          text: `{${settleKind}}`,
          at,
        })),
      ),
    ]),
    Match.tag('SettledLabel', ({ label }): ReadonlyArray<Fix> => [
      { title: 'Remove the `{To be settled}` label', text: '', at: label },
    ]),
    Match.tag('LabelBesideParameter', ({ label }): ReadonlyArray<Fix> => [
      { title: `Write \`${label}\` in full`, text: `kind="${label}"` },
    ]),
    Match.tag('StrayTarget', ({ target, at }): ReadonlyArray<Fix> => [
      { title: `Remove \`${target}\``, text: '', at },
    ]),
    Match.tag('NoSuchFile', ({ files, at }) =>
      Array.map(files, (file): Fix => ({ title: `Name \`${file}\``, text: `(${file})`, at })),
    ),
    Match.tag('SeveralFiles', ({ files, at }) =>
      Array.map(files, (file): Fix => ({ title: `Name \`${file}\``, text: `(${file})`, at })),
    ),
    Match.tag('TangleBesideSink', ({ specifier }): ReadonlyArray<Fix> => [
      { title: 'Remove `{Tangle}`', text: '', at: specifier },
    ]),
    Match.tag('ColonRun', (): ReadonlyArray<Fix> => [{ title: 'Write `:::`', text: ':::' }]),
    Match.tag('UnwrittenLoom', ({ kind, name, opening }): ReadonlyArray<Fix> => [
      {
        title: `Open the ${loomWords[kind].loom} \`${name}\``,
        text: opening.text,
        file: opening.file,
      },
    ]),
    Match.tag('StaleSummary', ({ kind, name, summary, at }): ReadonlyArray<Fix> => [
      { title: `Copy the ${loomWords[kind].copy} of \`${name}\``, text: summary, at },
    ]),
    Match.orElse((): ReadonlyArray<Fix> => []),
  )

const diagnosticOf = (note: Note, position: Position, fixes: ReadonlyArray<Fix>): Diagnostic =>
  Array.isReadonlyArrayNonEmpty(fixes)
    ? { message: note.message, position, severity: note.severity, fixes }
    : { message: note.message, position, severity: note.severity }

export const faultAt = (fault: LoomFault, position: Position): Diagnostic =>
  diagnosticOf(describe(fault), position, fixesOf(fault))

export const faulty = (fault: LoomFault, position: Position): Health => ({
  status: statusOf(describe(fault).severity),
  diagnostics: [faultAt(fault, position)],
})

## What this module draws on

`Data` builds the tagged union and a constructor for each fault, `Match` builds the exhaustive table, `Array` turns an ambiguous anchor's paths into its message and its fixes, and the keys and kinds that an anchor or a box takes into the messages of an unknown key and an unknown kind, and `Option` reads an unresolved anchor's declaration and the point where it can be settled. `@athrio/loom-ast` types what a fault becomes — the `Diagnostic` with its `Severity`, `Position`, and each `Fix` it offers, and the `Health` a node holds — and gives `settleKind`, the kind the settling fix writes, the `LoomKind` a box that points to a loom names, and `Opening`, the new loom an unwritten loom's fix writes.

=>

import { Array, Data, Match, Option } from 'effect'
import type { Diagnostic, Fix, Health, Position, Severity } from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomNode'
import { type Opening } from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomCorpusAst'
import { type LoomKind, settleKind } from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomTokens'

